## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design scalable, isolated multi-tenancy infrastructure using Terraform across multiple AWS accounts while leveraging a shared module registry, enforcing policy gates, and validating changes with automated tests.

## Key Concepts

- **Terraform provider aliases and multi-backend strategy**: Enabling isolated state management per tenant
- **Per-tenant resource isolation**: Dedicated VPCs, subnets, and security groups preventing cross-tenant access
- **Central module registry**: Single source of truth for tenant infrastructure configurations
- **CMK-based encryption for Redis**: Ensuring tenant data at rest is encrypted with customer-managed keys
- **CI policy gates for module updates**: Preventing production changes when shared dependencies are modified
- **Idempotence testing with Terratest**: Validating that Terraform configurations produce consistent, repeatable results

## Code Example

```hcl
# tenant-module/main.tf
variable "tenant_id" {}
variable "aws_account_id" {}

provider "aws" {
  alias = "tenant"
  assume_role {
    role_arn = "arn:aws:iam::${var.aws_account_id}:role/terraform-access"
  }
}

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    # Per-tenant bucket/key configured at runtime
  }
}

module "vpc" {
  source  = "app.terraform.io/org/vpc/aws"
  version = "2.0.0"

  providers = {
    aws = aws.tenant
  }

  tenant_id = var.tenant_id
  environment = "prod"
}

module "redis" {
  source  = "app.terraform.io/org/redis/aws"
  version = "1.5.0"

  providers = {
    aws = aws.tenant
  }

  tenant_id         = var.tenant_id
  cmk_key_arn       = var.cmk_arn
}
```

## Terratest Example

```go
func TestTenantRedisIdempotence(t *testing.T) {
  for _, tenant := range tenants {
    terraform.Init(t, &terraform.Options{TenantID: tenant})
    terraform.Apply(t, &terraform.Options{TenantID: tenant})

    plan := terraform.Plan(t, &terraform.Options{
      TenantID:  tenant,
      PlanOnly:  true,
    })

    require.Empty(t, plan.Changes, "Expected zero changes for %s", tenant)
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design environment-scoped Terraform with proper naming hygiene, state isolation, encryption, and CI validation gates - critical skills for production infrastructure.

## Key Concepts
- Wrapper module pattern for environment injection (dev/stage/prod)
- S3 bucket with server-side encryption (AES256) enabled by default
- CloudWatch log group for application logs
- Backend state per environment: state/${environment}/logs.tfstate
- CI gate enforcing naming convention (environment in bucket name) and encryption enforcement

## Complete Code Example

**variables.tf (wrapper)**
```hcl
variable "environment" {
  type        = string
  description = "Environment: dev, stage, or prod"
  validation {
    condition     = contains(["dev", "stage", "prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "Environment must be dev, stage, or prod."
  }
}
variable "project" {
  type        = string
  description = "Project name prefix"
}
variable "region" {
  type        = string
  default     = "us-east-1"
}
```

**main.tf (child module - s3_logs)**
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0"
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "logs" {
  bucket = "${var.project}-${var.environment}-logs"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "logs" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.logs.id

  rule {
    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
      sse_algorithm = "AES256"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_log_group" "app_logs" {
  name              = "/${var.project}/${var.environment}/logs"
  retention_in_days = var.environment == "prod" ? 90 : 7

  tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
  }
}

variable "project" { type = string }
variable "environment" { type = string }
```

**main.tf (wrapper)**
```hcl
module "logs_infrastructure" {
  source = "./modules/s3-logs"
  project     = var.project
  environment = var.environment
}
```

**backend.tf (partial configuration)**
```hcl
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    key            = "logs.tfstate"
    encrypt        = true
    bucket         = "my-terraform-state-bucket"
    dynamodb_table = "terraform-locks"
  }
}
```

**CI Gate - validate-terraform.yml**
```yaml
name: Terraform Validation
on: [pull_request]
jobs:
  validate:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3
        with:
          terraform_version: 1.6

      - name: Init terraform
        run: terraform init -backend-config="key=state/${{ matrix.environment }}/logs.tfstate"
        env:
          AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}
          AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}
        matrix:
          environment: [dev, stage, prod]

      - name: Validate bucket naming
        run: |
          BUCKET_NAME=$(terraform output -raw bucket_name)
          if [[ "$BUCKET_NAME" != *"${{ matrix.environment }}"* ]]; then
            echo "Error: Bucket name must contain environment: $BUCKET_NAME"
            exit 1
          fi

      - name: Validate encryption enabled
        run: |
          ENCRYPTION=$(terraform output -raw encryption_enabled)
          if [ "$ENCRYPTION" != "true" ]; then
            echo "Error: S3 bucket encryption must be enabled"
            exit 1
          fi
```

**outputs.tf**
```hcl
output "bucket_name" {
  value       = aws_s3_bucket.logs.id
  description = "Name of the S3 bucket for logs"
}

output "log_group_name" {
  value       = aws_cloudwatch_log_group.app_logs.name
  description = "Name of the CloudWatch log group"
}

output "encryption_enabled" {
  value       = true
  description = "Whether SSE encryption is enabled"
}
```

## Evaluation Criteria
- [ ] Wrapper module correctly injects environment context
- [ ] S3 bucket naming includes environment suffix
- [ ] Server-side encryption is enabled (AES256)
- [ ] CloudWatch log group created with appropriate retention
- [ ] Backend configured for per-environment state isolation
- [ ] CI gate validates bucket naming convention
- [ ] CI gate validates encryption is enabled

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-repo governance for infrastructure upgrades, ensuring drift prevention and auditable approval processes.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-repo data.terraform_remote_state consumption
- Per-environment version pinning strategies
- Upgrade gating mechanisms in CI/CD pipelines
- Open Policy Agent (OPA) policy enforcement

## Code Example

```javascript
// governance: remote state with allowed versions
data "terraform_remote_state" "versions" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "tf-governance-versions"
    key    = "versions/${var.env}/versions"
  }
}

// CI gate: plan and validate
resource "null_resource" "validate_upgrade" {
  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "terraform plan -out=tfplan && terraform show -json tfplan > plan.json"
  }
}

// OPA rule: enforce version compliance
package terraform.policy

default allow = false

allow {
  input.plan.configuration.provider_versions[p] == data.terraform_remote_state.versions.outputs.allowed_providers[p]
}
```

## Implementation Strategy

1. **Central Governance Repo**: Maintain version catalogs per environment in remote state
2. **Environment Repos**: Consume governance data via data.terraform_remote_state
3. **CI Integration**: Automated plan validation against approved versions
4. **OPA Enforcement**: Policy-as-code for upgrade compliance checking

This pattern ensures consistent upgrade governance across all infrastructure repositories while maintaining flexibility for environment-specific requirements.

